# Platform guardrails (policy-as-code)

The rules that protect every team environment, written as files, tested like
code, and changed only through an approved pull request.

| Folder | What is in it |
| --- | --- |
| `policies/` | The guardrails Kyverno enforces in every team namespace |
| `tests/` | The tests the pipeline runs on every change (`kyverno test tests/`) |
| `examples/` | A good deployment, a bad one, the exception lab's status page, and the exception template |
| `exceptions/` | Approved, time-limited exceptions ([how to ask](exceptions/README.md)) |

## The guardrails

| Policy | Rule in plain words |
| --- | --- |
| `pelab-trusted-registry` | Images must come from `{{ pelab_hosts.harbor }}`, where the pipeline put them after the checks and the scan |
| `pelab-immutable-tags` | Every image has an explicit tag, and never `latest` |
| `pelab-ownership-labels` | Every Deployment says what it is and which team owns it |
{% if golden_path_signing | bool %}
| `pelab-signed-images` | Team images carry the pipeline's signature (reported, not enforced) |
{% endif %}

Each refusal says which rule refused and how to fix it.

## Try it in your workspace

```bash
git clone https://{{ pelab_hosts.gitea }}/{{ golden_path_platform_org }}/policies && cd policies
kyverno apply policies/ --resource examples/good.yaml --values-file tests/values.yaml
kyverno apply policies/ --resource examples/bad.yaml --values-file tests/values.yaml
kyverno test tests/
```

`tests/values.yaml` tells the command line that `team01-dev` is a team namespace;
on the cluster Kyverno reads that from the namespace's labels.

On the cluster (replace team01 with your team):

```bash
sed 's/team01/teamNN/g' examples/bad.yaml | kubectl apply -f -
```

## How a change reaches the cluster

A pull request runs the tests. The platform team approves it. After the merge the
platform applies `main` within two minutes and shows the result as the
`platform/apply` status on the commit. No pipeline holds credentials that can
change the cluster.
